\v 32 and the other goldsmiths¦223133 and¦223134 some merchants¦223134 repaired¦223132 \add the last section of the wall\add* from the upper room in the corner¦223129 to the Sheep¦223131 Gate¦223130.
\c 4
\s1 The opposition to Nehemyah
\rem /s1 Nehemiah Overcomes Opposition to His Work; Opposition to the Rebuilding; Hostile Plots Thwarted; Enemies Oppose the Rebuilding
\p
\v 1 When¦223257 Sanballat¦223259 heard¦223258 that¦223264 we were rebuilding the wall¦223268, he¦223256 got really angry. He¦223258 mocked the Jews
\v 2 and¦223280 he spoke before \add ≈in¦223285 front\add* of his brothers and the army of Shomron \add !(Samaria)\add*, “What are those feeble Jews doing? \add ≈Do they think they'll be able to¦223283 rebuild that place\add* themselves? Will they sacrifice? Will they \add ≈get finished one\add* day? Will they form the stones \add ≈back together¦223282\add* from piles of dust, when they've been \add ≈weakened by the heat of the fires\add*?”
\p
\v 3 Toviyah the Ammonite was \add ≈standing beside him and¦223291 confirmed\add*, “Yes, even if a fox \add ≈walked over\add* that stone wall that they're building, it would make \add ≈it fall down\add*.”
\p
\v 4 \add I prayed,\add* “Our god, \add ≈listen to how we're mocked\add* and cause their \add ≈words\add* to¦223313 return on¦223314 their \add own\add* heads and \add ≈send them away as captives to become the¦223306 plunder for another country\add*.
\v 5 Don't¦223319 just cover over their evil, and¦223317 don't¦223319 \add ≈just ignore their sin\add*, because they've \add repeatedly\add* \add ≈made¦223334 the¦223329 workers get angry\add*.”
\p
\v 6 \add ≈So¦223341 we kept on at building\add* the wall, and¦223344 \add eventually\add* all¦223348 the \add segments of\add* wall were joined up together \add ≈up to about half its full height\add*, and \add so\add* the people \add ≈were encouraged to keep persevering\add*.
\p
\v 7 \add ≈Now\add* when Sanballat, and Toviyah, and the Arabians, and the Ammonites, and the Ashdodites heard that the Yerushalem walls¦223361 \add ≈were getting repaired\add* and¦223364 the¦223367 breaks were beginning to¦223368 be closed up, it \add ≈made them extremely angry\add*
\v 8 and they all got together to come to \add ≈attack¦223399\add* Yerushalem and \add ≈to throw us into confusion\add*.
\v 9 \add ≈So¦223421\add* we¦223427 prayed to our¦223414 god, \add ≈as well¦223415 as setting¦223415 up¦223415 a¦223415 24-hour¦223415 guard¦223415 to watch¦223415 out¦223415 for¦223415\add* them.
\p
\v 10 \add ≈People¦223444 around Yehudah¦223458\add* said, “The¦223441 \add ≈workers are running out of\add* strength and¦223447 there's \add ≈a lot of\add* rubble \add still to be moved\add*. \add ≈Maybe we're not up to building this\add* wall.”
\p
\v 11 Then \add ≈those¦223463 against us\add* said, “\add ≈We'll rush in¦223462 among them and¦223463 kill some of¦223466 them before they even see us or know that we're coming\add*, then \add ≈that will bring the¦223472 work¦223469 to an end\add*.”
\v 12 \add ≈After\add* that¦223483, whenever the¦223475 Jews living close to them came, they¦223482 \add ≈repeatedly warned us\add*, “\add ≈They're going to attack us from all directions\add*.”
\v 13 \add ≈So\add* I stationed \add guards\add* behind the lowest places along the wall¦223506 \add ≈and where there were still gaps\add*. I \add also\add* \add ≈made¦223499 sure that the families stood guard\add* with their swords, spears, and bows.
\p
\v 14 I looked then I \add ≈got organised\add* and told the nobles and the prefects, and the rest of¦223517 the¦223518 people, “Don't be afraid of¦223522 their \add ≈threats\add*. Remember my \add ≈powerful\add* and fearsome master, and fight¦223523 for¦223524 your brothers, your sons and your daughters, your wives, and your houses.”
\v 15 \add ≈After all that\add*, when¦223533 our enemies \add ≈realised that we were¦223528 aware of their intentions and¦223530 that God had frustrated their surprise attack plan\add*, \add ≈we all returned to our work¦223529 on¦223529\add* the¦223534 wall.
\p
\v 16 \add ≈So from that¦223542 day¦223555 onwards\add*, half of¦223546 my young men¦223544 would¦223550 \add ≈work¦223556 on¦223541 the rebuilding, while the other half stood guard¦223554 with¦223541\add* spears, shields, and breastplates, and the¦223547 officials \add ≈encouraged¦223547 them¦223547 all¦223547 from behind¦223547\add*.
\v 17 \add Both\add* those who were \add ≈placing the stones on\add* the wall and¦223559 those who¦223565 were carrying the heavy loads \add to them\add*, \add always\add* \add ≈had their weapons¦223573 with them\add* \add in case of an attack\add*.
\v 18 The builders \add ≈had their swords strapped to their sides as they worked\add*, and \add ≈the man who would sound the trumpet stayed with\add* me.
\v 19 Then I told the nobles and prefects, and the rest of the people, “\add ≈This job is so large and extensive that we're all separated from each other when we work on\add* the wall,
\v 20 \add so\add* whenever you all hear the trumpet blast, join us at that place. Our god will fight for us.”
\v 21 \add ≈So\add* we \add ≈continued the work with\add* half of them keeping their hands on their spears from the \add ≈beginning of the day\add* until the stars came out \add at night\add*.
\p
\v 22 Also at that time, I told the people, “\add ≈Every worker including servants should spend the night inside the city, so they double as workers during the day and guards at\add* night.”
\v 23 \add During that period of construction,\add* none of us, not even our servants, \add ≈stayed undressed\add* or \add ≈separated from our weapons, even when washing\add*.
\c 5
\s1 The resolution of Nehemyah of problem
\rem /s1 Oppression of the Poor; Nehemiah Defends the Oppressed; Nehemiah Deals with Oppression; Nehemiah's social reforms; Nehemiah Helps the Poor
\p
\v 1 \add ≈However¦223585\add* \add around then,\add* there was a \add ≈widespread complaint from\add* the¦223578 people¦223579 and¦223580 their wives¦223580 against¦223582 \add ≈what their¦223584 fellow Jews¦223585 were doing\add*.
\v 2 \add ≈Some of them complained\add*, “\add ≈There's¦223587 a¦223587 lot¦223587 of us¦223587 when¦223587 we include¦223587\add* our¦223590 sons¦223590 and¦223591 daughters¦223591. \add ≈We¦223596 need\add* grain¦223595 so¦223597 we can eat¦223596 and¦223597 \add ≈stay alive¦223597\add*.”
\p
\v 3 \add ≈Others\add* said¦223601, “We¦223605 are¦223606 mortgaging¦223606 our fields¦223602 and¦223603 vineyards¦223603, and our houses¦223604 so we¦223605 can get¦223607 grain¦223608 during¦223609 this famine¦223609.”
\p
\v 4 \add ≈Yet others\add* said¦223613, “We've borrowed¦223614 silver \add to pay the tax\add* on our fields¦223618 and¦223611 our¦223618 vineyards¦223619 \add ≈that¦223612 goes to the¦223616 tribute¦223616 to be paid to\add* the¦223617 \add foreign\add* king¦223617.
\v 5 \add ≈We're Jews just like¦223622 our¦223623 countrymen¦223623, but¦223621 we're¦223628 having to¦223636 commit our children¦223625 into¦223629 bondage as¦223625 slaves¦223636—yes, even\add* our daughters¦223635. \add ≈Now¦223621 we¦223628 own nothing at all—not¦223640 even\add* our¦223642 fields¦223643 and¦223621 vineyards¦223644.
\p
\v 6 I got very¦223649 angry when I heard¦223651 their¦223654 \add ≈complaints and¦223655 desperation\add*,
\v 7 and¦223659 \add ≈I couldn't get it¦223659 out of¦223660 my¦223660 mind¦223660\add*. I \add ≈protested\add* to¦223670 the nobles¦223665 and¦223666 the¦223668 officials¦223668, “You guys are¦223677 \add ≈charging interest¦223671 against¦223682 your own countrymen\add*.”\x + \xo 5:7: \xt Exo 22:25; Lev 25:35-37; Deu 23:19-20.\x*
\p \add ≈So¦223659 I¦223662 called¦223681 for¦223670 a gathering¦223683 of all the¦223665 people to deal with¦223675\add* them¦223682,
\v 8 and¦223686 I told¦223686 them¦223687, “Our¦223692 brothers, the¦223693 Jews¦223693, were sold¦223694 to¦223695 \add other\add* countries¦223695 \add as slaves\add* \add ≈and we've¦223689 done all we can to bring them back here\add*. But¦223686 now even you yourselves are buying¦223689 and¦223698 selling¦223701 your own countrymen¦223704 \add to make a profit\add*.” They¦223708 \add ≈couldn't argue against that, so they¦223710 were just\add* silent¦223708.
\p
\v 9 “It's not¦223718 good¦223720 what you're doing¦223725,” I¦223714 \add ≈continued¦223714\add*. “Shouldn't you all \add ≈respect¦223727 and¦223714 obey\add* our¦223728 god¦223728, because \add ≈otherwise we'll be despised by the¦223721 other\add* nations¦223731, our enemies¦223732.
\v 10 I¦223736 myself have lent¦223739 them¦223740 money¦223741 and¦223742 grain¦223742, as have my brothers¦223737 and¦223738 my¦223738 young men, \add but\add* please¦223745 let's¦223743 forgo charging interest¦223748.
\v 11 Please¦223752, even today, return¦223751 their fields¦223755 to¦223753 them¦223753, and their¦223755 vineyards¦223756 and olive¦223757 orchards, their houses¦223758, and¦223759 \add ≈the¦223760 interest on¦223767\add* the money¦223760 and¦223761 the¦223761 grain¦223761, the¦223762 new¦223762 wine¦223762, and¦223763 the¦223763 oil¦223763 that¦223764 you¦223765 are¦223766 charging against them¦223767.”
\p
\v 12 “\add ≈Okay, we'll¦223770 do¦223775 what you're¦223777 asking¦223769\add*,” they¦223769 \add ≈responded¦223769\add*. “We'll return¦223770 \add >their¦223770 property¦223770\add* and¦223783 we \add ≈won't¦223772 impose any more charges on\add* them¦223771.”
\p \add ≈So¦223769\add* I¦223779 called¦223779 the¦223782 priests¦223782 \add ≈to witness those nobles and officials making¦223783 an¦223783 oath¦223783 to do what they'd said¦223769 they would do¦223784\add*.
\v 13 Also¦223788 I¦223791 shook¦223791 \add ≈the¦223795 folds¦223790 of¦223790 my¦223790 robe¦223790\add* and¦223792 told¦223792 \add >them¦223792\add*, “May¦223794 God shake¦223794 you all¦223816 out of his¦223809 house¦223809 like that, and \add ≡shake\add* \add ≈every¦223798 man's¦223800 business if he doesn't do what he just¦223808 promised¦223826\add*—may¦223812 he¦223812 be¦223812 shaken¦223813 out¦223813 like that and \add ≈lose everything\add*.”
\rem /s1 The Generosity of Nehemiah; Nehemiah's Unselfishness
\s1 Nehemyah's generosity
\p
\v 14 Also¦223829, from¦223830 the¦223830 day¦223830 that I was¦223836 appointed¦223834 to¦223836 be¦223836 their¦223837 governor in¦223838 the Yehudah¦223839 region¦223838 (for the¦223847 twelve years¦223848 from \add the Persian\add* King¦223847 Artahshashta's¦223846 20th year until his 32nd year), I myself didn't¦223855 \add ≈accept the governor's¦223854 food¦223853 allowance\add*, nor did my¦223852 \add ≈relatives¦223852\add*.
\v 15 The¦223858 governors¦223858 who¦223860 preceded me had been very \add ≈hard¦223863\add* on¦223869 the people—\add ≈demanding\add* bread¦223869 and¦223870 wine¦223870 and forty¦223875 silver¦223872 coins. Even their¦223877 servants¦223877 \add ≈oppressed\add* the¦223881 people¦223881. \add In contrast,\add* I¦223882 didn't¦223883 do¦223885 that \add ≈due to desire to honour\add* God¦223889.
\v 16 Also¦223891 I¦223895 \add ≈remained focused on¦223892 rebuilding the\add* wall¦223893, and¦223896 we¦223898 \add ≈didn't¦223897 invest in¦223892 a¦223896 farm property\add*, and¦223899 all¦223899 my¦223901 servants¦223901 \add ≈also¦223896 contributed\add* to the¦223906 work¦223906.
\v 17 \add ≈I regularly fed a hundred¦223910 and¦223911 fifty¦223911 Jewish leaders¦223909 and city officials¦223909, as well as visitors from¦223915 other countries¦223917\add* around¦223920 us¦223920,
\v 18 \add ≈so in¦223943 one day¦223928 we needed\add* one¦223929 bull¦223930, six¦223933 choice sheep¦223932, \add ≈and¦223925 various kinds of poultry¦223936\add*, as well as various types of¦223941 wines¦223945 every ten¦223941 days¦223942. \add ≈I¦223953 didn't¦223952 demand any of this¦223949 from the¦223950 people¦223960 because¦223954 it¦223956 would have been¦223956 a heavy¦223956 burden¦223957 for¦223954 them\add*.
\mi
\v 19 My¦223966 god¦223966, remember¦223963 me¦223965 for¦223967 the¦223974 good¦223967 that¦223975 I've¦223971 done¦223971 for these people¦223974.
\c 6
\s1 The plan of killing Nehemyah
\rem /s1 Continued Opposition to Rebuilding; Intrigues of Enemies Foiled; Plots against Nehemiah; Further Opposition to the Rebuilding
\p
\v 1 Although we didn't yet have¦223988 any doors¦224002 in¦224006 the gates¦224006, when Sanballat¦223981 and¦223982 Toviyah¦223982 and¦223983 Geshem¦223983 the¦223984 Arabian¦223984 and¦223985 the¦223985 rest¦223985 of¦223985 our¦223986 enemies¦223986 heard¦223980 that¦223987 we'd finished building¦223988 the¦223991 wall¦223991 so it¦223994 didn't have any gaps left¦223994 in¦223995 it¦223995,
\v 2 Sanballat¦224009 and¦224008 Geshem¦224010 \add ≈invited\add* me¦224011, “We'd¦224016 like to arrange to meet¦224014 with¦224016 you in¦224016 one of¦224017 the villages¦224016 on¦224016 the¦224017 Ono¦224018 plain¦224017.” \add ≈But¦224008\add* they¦224019 were¦224020 \add ≈intending to¦224022 harm¦224023\add* me.
\v 3 \add ≈So¦224025\add* I sent¦224025 messengers¦224027 \add back\add* to them¦224026, “\add ≈This work¦224029 is¦224032 important and¦224033 extensive, so I can't¦224033 afford to abandon it¦224037 and have the work stop¦224037 if I travelled down¦224041 to¦224042 you on the plain\add*.”
\p
\v 4 They¦224044 \add ≈tried four¦224048 times¦224049 to get me¦224045 to go, but¦224044 I¦224050 kept refusing them¦224051 for those¦224051 same¦224053 reasons\add*.
\p
\v 5 Then Sanballat¦224058 sent¦224056 his¦224065 servant¦224065 to¦224057 me¦224057 a¦224061 fifth¦224062 time \add ≈with the¦224059 same request, but¦224056 this¦224060 time¦224061 in¦224068 an unsealed, open¦224067 letter¦224066\add*
\v 6 saying¦224075, “\add ≈There's rumours flying around in¦224071 nearby\add* countries¦224072, and¦224074 Geshem¦224074 \add ≈confirms it¦224071\add*, that you¦224076 and¦224077 the¦224077 Jews¦224077 are¦224078 building¦224084 the¦224085 wall¦224085 because you're¦224083 all plotting thinking to rebel¦224079. Then you'd become¦224087 their king¦224089, according¦224090 to \add ≈what we're hearing¦224073\add*.
\v 7 \add ≈We've also¦224093 heard that\add* you've¦224096 set up ‘prophets¦224095’ to speak¦224106 out in Yerushalem¦224099 to¦224100 say¦224100 about you, ‘There's a¦224101 king¦224101 in¦224102 Yehudah¦224102.’ By now¦224103, all of that will¦224104 have¦224096 been heard¦224104 by the¦224105 \add Persian\add* king. \add ≈So¦224093\add* now¦224108, come¦224109 and¦224093 let's¦224110 \add ≈discuss this\add* together¦224111.”
\p
\v 8 I¦224114 sent¦224114 \add ≈a message¦224119 straight back\add*, “\add ≈None of¦224125 what you're¦224122 saying¦224123 is true. It's¦224115 simply your¦224125 own imagination at work\add*.”
\p
\v 9 \add We knew that\add* they¦224135 were¦224132 just trying to¦224134 frighten¦224132 us¦224133 \add ≈so that we'd stop the¦224139 repairs and¦224140 it¦224141 would¦224141 never get finished\add*.
\mi \add My god,\add* strengthen¦224143 my¦224146 hands¦224136.
\p
\v 10 \add After that,\add* I entered Shemayah's¦224152 house (the¦224153 son¦224153 of¦224153 Mehetav'el's son Delayah¦224155) who was¦224160 confined \add to his house\add*, and¦224148 he¦224159 said¦224161, “Let's¦224162 set a time to meet¦224162 in the temple—we'll shut¦224160 the temple¦224170 doors¦224172 because¦224174 they'll be¦224175 coming¦224175 to¦224176 kill¦224176 you¦224176—probably in the night¦224177.”
\p
\v 11 \add ≈But¦224181 I disagreed\add*, “Would a man¦224182 like¦224183 me¦224183 run¦224184 away? \add ≈Besides that, who's¦224185 allowed to¦224190 live¦224193 inside the¦224192 temple¦224192\add*? I won't¦224194 \add ≈do¦224185 that¦224187\add*.”
\p
\v 12 \add ≈Then¦224197 I¦224198 suddenly realised that God¦224201 hadn't told¦224205 him¦224202 to say that, but¦224197 rather he was working against¦224206 me¦224206 because¦224203 he'd been paid by\add* Toviyah¦224207 and¦224197 Sanballat¦224208.
\v 13 \add ≈They¦224226 must¦224220 have hired¦224212 him\add* so¦224214 that¦224214 I'd be¦224216 afraid¦224216 and¦224217 \add ≈take that advice\add* and¦224220 sin¦224220. \add ≈Then they¦224226 could¦224216 have ruined my reputation¦224223 and discredited\add* me¦224226.
\mi
\v 14 My¦224230 god¦224230, remember¦224229 what Toviyah¦224231 and¦224232 Sanballat¦224232 have done, and¦224235 also¦224235 the¦224237 prophetess¦224237 Noadyah¦224236 and¦224238 the other prophets¦224239 who've been¦224241 \add ≈trying to¦224231 scare me¦224243\add*.
\s1 The completion of wall
\rem /s1 The Conclusion of the Work; The Builders Complete the Wall; The Completion of the Wall; The Wall Completed
\p
\v 15 \add ≈So¦224245\add* \add the rebuilding of\add* the¦224246 wall¦224246 was¦224245 finished¦224245 on¦224247 the 25th of¦224249 September¦224249 in¦224247 \add just\add* fifty-two¦224250 days¦224252,
\v 16 and¦224255 when our enemies¦224260 heard¦224257 \add about it\add*, the¦224264 surrounding¦224266 countries¦224264 were¦224266 afraid \add ≈and discouraged\add* because¦224271 they¦224267 realised¦224261 that¦224265 our god¦224273 \add ≈must have¦224274 helped us¦224266\add*.
\p
\v 17 \add ≈During¦224280 that¦224286 time¦224280\add*, the¦224280 Yehudah¦224284 nobles¦224283 were¦224282 \add ≈sending many\add* letters¦224285 to¦224287 Toviyah, and¦224290 \add ≡letters\add* from Toviyah were¦224292 \add also\add* coming¦224292 to¦224293 them¦224293,
\v 18 because¦224295 many¦224297 \add people\add* in¦224298 Yehudah¦224298 \add ≈had ties with¦224298 him¦224301\add* because¦224302 he¦224305 was¦224304 the¦224307 son-in-law¦224304 of¦224307 Arah's¦224309 son Shekanyah¦224306, plus¦224310 his¦224311 son Yehohanan¦224310 had¦224312 \add ≈married\add* Berekyah's¦224319 daughter¦224315 Meshullam¦224317.
\v 19 \add @Those¦224322 nobles¦224322\add* kept telling¦224324 me \add ≈about¦224322 the good¦224322 things he¦224331 was doing\add*, and¦224326 they¦224323 reported my¦224325 words¦224326 \add back\add* to¦224329 him¦224329. Toviyah¦224332 \add also\add* sent¦224331 letters¦224330 to¦224333 \add try to\add* frighten¦224333 me¦224333.
\c 7
\p
\v 1 \add ≈Once¦224339\add* the wall¦224338 had been rebuilt¦224337 and¦224339 the¦224340 doors¦224340 \add ≈hung\add* in the gates, the gatekeepers¦224342 and¦224343 the¦224343 singers¦224343 and¦224344 the¦224344 Levites¦224344 had been appointed¦224341.
\v 2 I put my¦224350 brother¦224350 Hanani¦224349 and¦224351 the¦224354 fortress¦224355 commander¦224354 Hananyah in charge¦224346 over¦224356 Yerushalem¦224358. (Hananyah was¦224362 a faithful¦224363 man¦224362 and¦224364 \add ≈respected¦224364\add* God more¦224368 than¦224368 many¦224368 \add others do\add*.)
\v 3 I told¦224370 them¦224372, “Don't¦224373 open¦224374 Yerushalem's¦224376 gates¦224375 until¦224377 \add ≈broad daylight\add*, and then¦224370 shut¦224384 and bar them again while \add ≈the guards¦224383 are¦224383 still on full alert\add*. And¦224393 \add ≈assign¦224387 neighbourhood watch groups among the inhabitants¦224389 to¦224372 keep an eye on their¦224395 own areas\add*.”
\s1 The list of returned exiles
\rem /s1 The List of Those Who Returned from Exile; The List of the Exiles Who Returned; Nehemiah Registers the People
\r (Ezra 2:1-70)
\p
\v 4 \add ≈Now\add* Yerushalem \add ≈covered a large¦224400 area but¦224397 not¦224404 many people¦224401 had¦224406 returned to live there yet, and¦224397 the¦224397 houses¦224405 hadn't yet been¦224406 rebuilt¦224406\add*.
\rem /s1 Lists of the Returned Exiles
\p
\v 5 \add ≈So¦224408\add* God¦224409 \add ≈gave¦224408 me the idea of\add* gathering the \add ≈leaders¦224419 and¦224408 the people¦224422 grouped by their ancestors\add*. I¦224413 found¦224424 the¦224425 \add ≈register of¦224425 those¦224427 who'd returned¦224427\add* \add from exile to Yerushalem\add*, with¦224428 this written¦224430 in¦224428 it¦224431:
\p
\v 6 “These¦224434 are¦224436 the¦224436 descendants¦224436 of¦224436 \add ≈the people exiled¦224440 by the¦224444 Babylonian King¦224444 Nevukadnetstsar, who¦224438 returned¦224446 to Yehudah¦224448 and¦224448 to Yerushalem¦224447—each family returning¦224446 to their own¦224450 ancestral town¦224450\add*.
\v 7 \add ≈Their leaders were\add* Zerubavel¦224455, Yeshua¦224456, Nehemyah¦224457, Azaryah¦224458, Raamyah¦224459, Nahamani¦224460, Mareddekai, Bilshan¦224462, Misperet¦224463, Bigvai¦224464, Nehum¦224465, and Baanah¦224466.
\p The¦224467 number¦224467 of¦224468 Israeli¦224469 men¦224468:
\li1
\v 8 2,172 descendants¦224473 of¦224473 Far'osh¦224474,
\li1
\v 9 372 descendants¦224481 of¦224481 Shefatyah¦224482,
\li1
\v 10 652 descendants¦224489 of¦224489 Arah¦224490,
\li1
\v 11 2,818 descendants¦224497 of¦224497 Pahat¦224499-Moav¦224500 (descendants¦224501 of Yeshua¦224502 and¦224503 Yoav¦224503),
\li1
\v 12 1,254 descendants¦224511 of¦224511 Eylam¦224512,
\li1
\v 13 845 descendants¦224519 of¦224519 Zattu¦224520,
\li1
\v 14 760 descendants¦224527 of¦224527 Zakkay¦224528,
\li1
\v 15 648 descendants¦224534 of¦224534 Binnuy¦224535,
\li1
\v 16 628 descendants¦224542 of¦224542 Bevai¦224543,
\li1
\v 17 2,322 descendants¦224550 of¦224550 Azgad¦224551,
\li1
\v 18 667 descendants¦224559 of¦224559 Adonikam¦224560,
\li1
\v 19 2,067 descendants¦224567 of¦224567 Bigvai¦224568,
\li1
\v 20 655 descendants¦224574 of¦224574 Adin¦224575,
\li1
